{"bundle_id":"population.w344-open-problem-p0023-checked-partials-20261010","entries":[{"key":"symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","logical_id":"math:probability.reflected_brownian:symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","revision":{"assumptions":["A global reflection symmetry of the triangle","Starting points are mirror images","Generator Delta/2"],"conditions":[],"disposition_reason":"","domain":"probability theory","evidence_ids":[],"kind":"lemma","logical_id":"math:probability.reflected_brownian:symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","metadata":{"draft":"w344/bundle_003.json","proof_text":"Burdzy's cached official open-problems page, Problem 4, asks about arbitrary distinct starting points in any acute triangle. The prior attempt gives only a spectral lower bound, with the correct generator convention. For a bounded reflection-symmetric triangle D, let R be reflection through a symmetry axis and start at x and Rx, with x off the axis. Run Neumann reflected Brownian motion X with generator Delta/2, set Y=RX until the first hitting time T of the axis, and set Y=X thereafter. Reflection invariance of the domain and the strong Markov property make both marginals reflected Brownian motion. At T, X=RX, so the construction is coalescent.

Before T, X lives in one half-triangle with Neumann condition on its exterior sides and absorbing condition on the axis. Its survival exponent is the first mixed-boundary eigenvalue lambda_mix of -Delta/2. Odd extension across the axis sends every admissible half-domain test function to a mean-zero H^1 function on D, preserving its Rayleigh quotient. Thus lambda_mix>=lambda_1, where lambda_1 is the first positive full-domain Neumann generator eigenvalue. The bounded-domain killed heat semigroup therefore gives, for each gamma<lambda_1, P_x(T>t)<=C_(x,gamma) exp(-gamma t). This is an affirmative special case for mirror-image starting points in reflection-symmetric acute triangles. It does not construct a coupling for arbitrary pairs or scalene triangles.

Checks: require a global domain symmetry, not just a reflected noise formula; identify absorbing/reflecting boundary pieces; require coalescence after meeting; use -Delta/2 rather than -Delta. A one-dimensional reflection control on [0,1], starting at 1/4 and 3/4, has generator exponent pi^2/2. Its exact mixed-boundary survival expansion has scaled limit (4/pi)cos(pi/4); three values are saved in special_case_measurements.jsonl. That control checks the convention and stopping mechanism, not the full triangular spectral theory."},"provenance":{"imported_by":"w344","locator":"urn:mb-local:f530f9044eb0a47f","source_content_digest":"sha256:69ccc74aef3102a44dc17fc6fdf77eb2639adb778d6df5e826cb58dffeff252f","source_id":"owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","source_revision":"2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"trust_class":"curated_secondary"},"schema_version":1,"statement":"Let D be a bounded triangle invariant under reflection R across an axis, and let lambda_1 be the first positive Neumann eigenvalue of -Delta/2 on D. For starting points x and Rx with x off the axis, there is a coalescent coupling of reflected Brownian motions with generator Delta/2 whose meeting time T satisfies P(T>t)<=C_(x,gamma) exp(-gamma t) for every 0<gamma<lambda_1. The coupling reflects one path across the axis until it hits that axis, then uses the same path. The killed half-domain principal eigenvalue is at least lambda_1 by odd extension and the Neumann Rayleigh principle. In particular, the result covers mirror starting pairs in reflection-symmetric acute triangles, not arbitrary pairs or scalene triangles.","status":"unverified","supersedes":[],"title":"Efficient reflected Brownian coupling for mirror starting pairs in a symmetric triangle"},"revision_digest":"sha256:08b3ac4141660dadad65bad58d200aa2b127bb34988304c484200d49180d7c21","revision_id":"claim_08b3ac4141660dadad65bad58d200aa2b127bb34988304c484200d49180d7c21"}],"metadata":{"author_agent":"w344","draft_file":"w344/bundle_003.json","verification_disposition":"population-phase content, trust CURATED_SECONDARY, pending QA"},"relations":[{"kind":"specializes","metadata":{},"rationale":"New end: "Let D be a bounded triangle invariant under reflection R across an axis, and let lambda_1 be the"; existing end: "In any acute triangle, can reflected Brownian motions from distinct starting points be coupled so their meeting-time tail". This result addresses or audits P0023; the cited card identifies its mathematical context and is not used as a proof or a current openness certificate.","source":{"scope":"bundle_entry","value":"symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling"},"target":{"scope":"existing_node","value":"claim_9e17396f720cd1e4e827ae0d713f609daf23cf32c02a5e988ea45e29e35ee4a4"},"weight":1.0}],"release_manifest":{"approved_by":"population-gate claude-mathbrain-build-0802","authorization_key_id":"population-gate-20260802","authorization_mac":"hmac-sha256:a751705e8f8f62c853970c2f942dda69f27b849dde06f0034432b679ff5d9279","claim_bindings":[{"entry_key":"symmetric-triangle-mirror-start-efficient-coupling","proof_digest":"sha256:9cc54fd3c5103f04f87643d593c2c08dd0cab53fc27c2029ed7bdccb02c54e4a","proof_locator":"urn:mb-local:f530f9044eb0a47f","source_artifact_ids":["c608379928f6137f70e7e99ebf269bcb"],"statement_digest":"sha256:eb596177b1575e070d036080a9b0d91142547a7832a7dff0110a9fac75b820e7","statement_locator":"urn:mb-local:f530f9044eb0a47f"}],"core":{"allowlist_manifest_digest":"sha256:36cfc3bd822a077fbb4f36c2a7802f9c3ddb26d559cdda1e78a4e6a5f74da724","builder_digest":"sha256:18491fd3e470fc2d00d17fc1965e042887f35f10b8b37a0ca7dc26872cbba6bb","math_brain_package_digest":"sha256:22b0620148224e5ee9ee015706c62045037c14907c3fd70d38137624d271e8cb","normalization_digest":"sha256:78c78aa3d0ec358c34ec4bf576ae593f315b040eb8442288629f4ec9354bcf0f","proofswarm_parser_digest":"s